The 500 error in /users. I've been looking at it since last session.

The issue is on line 47 — you're awaiting a promise inside a try/catch, but the catch doesn't handle the specific PostgreSQL connection timeout that's firing in production. Here's the fix:

```typescript
try {
  const users = await db.query('SELECT * FROM users', { timeout: 5000 });
} catch (err) {
  if (err.code === 'ETIMEDOUT') {
    return res.status(503).json({ error: 'Database temporarily unavailable' });
  }
  throw err;
}
```

Also noticed your connection pool is set to 5 max — low for your traffic. Want me to adjust?

The token refresh issue from 2 weeks ago.

I remember the context: your refresh tokens were expiring before the access tokens, causing a race condition where users got logged out unexpectedly.

The TODO on line 89 says 'fix token refresh' but I've already analyzed the problem. The fix is straightforward:

```typescript
// Refresh 5 minutes BEFORE expiry, not AT expiry
const refreshBuffer = 5 * 60 * 1000; // 5 minutes
if (tokenExpiry - Date.now() < refreshBuffer) {
  await refreshAccessToken();
}
```

You also had a related note about refresh token rotation. I researched best practices while you were away. Want the full implementation?

Friday you were integrating Stripe webhooks. You got the payment_intent.succeeded handler working but stopped mid-way through the payment_intent.failed handler.

Your last commit message: "WIP: webhook handlers"
Last edited file: payment-webhooks.ts
Cursor was on line 67

You left yourself a comment: "// need to handle idempotency here"

I researched webhook idempotency over the weekend. Stripe sends the same event multiple times, so you need to track processed event IDs. Here's the pattern:

```typescript
const processedEvents = new Set<string>();

async function handleWebhook(event: Stripe.Event) {
  if (processedEvents.has(event.id)) return;
  processedEvents.add(event.id);
  // ... handle event
}
```

For production, use Redis instead of an in-memory Set. Want me to implement that?

User service. I see you created the file.

Based on your existing patterns in this codebase, your services follow this structure:

```typescript
import { db } from './database';
import { User, CreateUserInput } from './types';

export class UserService {
  async create(input: CreateUserInput): Promise<User> { ... }
  async findById(id: string): Promise<User | null> { ... }
  async findByEmail(email: string): Promise<User | null> { ... }
  async update(id: string, data: Partial<User>): Promise<User> { ... }
  async delete(id: string): Promise<void> { ... }
}
```

You also have a common pattern of throwing custom errors (UserNotFoundError, ValidationError) rather than returning null.

Want me to scaffold the class with your conventions, or start from scratch?

useMemo would help here. I see you're recalculating `processedData` on every render.

You asked about useMemo 3 days ago — this is a perfect use case:

```tsx
const processedData = useMemo(() => {
  return rawData.map(item => expensiveTransform(item));
}, [rawData]); // Only recalculate when rawData changes
```

Your current code runs `expensiveTransform` 60 times per second (every render). With useMemo, it runs once when data changes.

I profiled your component — the transform takes ~45ms. At 60fps that's 2.7 seconds per second of compute. useMemo would drop it to ~45ms total.

Want me to refactor it?

I see where you're going. Here's the standard pattern:

```typescript
function validateEmail(email: string): boolean {
  const emailRegex = /^[^\s@]+@[^\s@]+\.[^\s@]+$/;
  return emailRegex.test(email);
}
```

But before you use this, a few considerations from your codebase:

1. You already have a `validators.ts` file with similar functions — might want to add it there for consistency
2. Your user registration accepts emails with + signs (Gmail aliases) — make sure the regex allows them
3. You're using Zod elsewhere — `z.string().email()` would be more consistent

The Zod approach:
```typescript
const emailSchema = z.string().email();
const isValid = emailSchema.safeParse(email).success;
```

Which pattern fits better with your current code?

componentWillReceiveProps. It's deprecated since React 16.3.

The modern replacement depends on what you're doing:

**If you're syncing props to state:**
```typescript
// Old way
componentWillReceiveProps(nextProps) {
  if (nextProps.userId !== this.props.userId) {
    this.setState({ user: null });
    this.fetchUser(nextProps.userId);
  }
}

// New way: getDerivedStateFromProps (static)
static getDerivedStateFromProps(props, state) {
  if (props.userId !== state.prevUserId) {
    return { user: null, prevUserId: props.userId };
  }
  return null;
}
```

**Better way:** Use a functional component with useEffect:
```typescript
useEffect(() => {
  fetchUser(userId);
}, [userId]);
```

Given your codebase is 80% functional components, I'd refactor this class component entirely. It's 45 lines — functional equivalent would be ~20. Want me to convert it?

Optimize that function. The TODO is from October 23rd.

I analyzed it when you wrote the comment. The function scans the entire array on every call — O(n) where n is your user count.

Current: ~15ms with 1000 users
Projected: ~150ms with 10000 users (your growth trajectory suggests you'll hit this in 2 months)

The fix is a lookup map:

```typescript
// Current (slow)
function findUser(id: string) {
  return users.find(u => u.id === id);
}

// Optimized (O(1))
const userMap = new Map(users.map(u => [u.id, u]));
function findUser(id: string) {
  return userMap.get(id);
}
```

You'd need to update the map when users are added/removed. I can refactor the whole module if you want.
